qsm-pve-direct for Proxmox VE 9
===============================

`qsm-pve-direct` is a browser-native PVE console transport. Install it as the
sole non-stock console transport on the node.

It uses PVE's existing authenticated Web UI session and `VM.Console` ACL.
The browser sends a WebRTC SDP offer to a protected PVE route; the node-local
terminal answers it and starts one QEMU Display1 worker.  H.264/Opus remain
encoded until the browser's WebRTC implementation. There is no pairing PIN,
native client installer, external listening port, or host desktop server.

Install:

    sudo apt install ./qsm-pve-direct_*.deb
    sudo systemctl enable --now qsm-pve-direct-terminal.service

The service reads PVE's saved VM configuration and creates the private
Display1 bus before the VM is started. No per-VM policy file is required for
the portable `libx264` default. After saving a Display1 setting, the service
reconciles the bus automatically. Package upgrades may restart the terminal
service, but preserve every live private Display1 bus: a running configured
VM does not need to be rebooted solely for this package update.

In the PVE VM's **Hardware → Display → Advanced**, enable **QSM Display1**.
It disables PVE's VNC display for that VM, then writes one private D-Bus
Display1 argument and one VirtIO-GPU (VirGL) device. PVE VNC cannot coexist
with QEMU's GL Display1 backend; use QSM Direct as the VM's browser console
while this setting is enabled. Reopening the dialog restores the checkbox and
render node from the saved arguments. **QSM Direct** in the Console menu is
enabled only for a VM with that exact Display1 configuration. Start the VM,
then choose the enabled menu item. Resizing the console window or entering/leaving
fullscreen sends the selected even viewport to QEMU Display1 and causes a
fresh H.264 configuration/IDR boundary.

An optional root-owned `/etc/qsm-pve-direct/instances.d/<VMID>.conf` (0600)
selects a node-specific encoder without changing the display setup:

    QSM_DIRECT_QEMU_DBUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/qsm-pve-direct/<VMID>/qemu-display1.bus
    QSM_DIRECT_ENCODER=h264_nvenc

For VA-API additionally set
`QSM_DIRECT_VAAPI_RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/renderD128`.

The direct WebRTC console provides video, guest audio, keyboard, pointer,
scrolling, dynamic Display1 resolution, and optional guest clipboard/file
exchange. For the latter install the matching `qsm-guest-agent` package inside
the Linux VM, then run `sudo qsm-guest-agent-setup USER` once for its logged-in
desktop user. On Wayland, also enable `qsm-guest-clipboard.service` in that
user's session.

The browser's **Files** panel lists only
`~/.local/share/qsm-guest-agent/outgoing`; place a guest file there to make it
available for Download. Dragging a local file directly onto the console image
stores it in the matching `incoming` directory. Chromium can prepare an
outgoing item for host drag-out; Download is the portable browser path because
a website is not permitted to write an arbitrary local filesystem path without
the user's consent. Clipboard is UTF-8 text up to 1 MiB; each exchange file is
limited to 2 MiB and must have a plain safe basename. Neither the console nor
the agent exposes arbitrary guest filesystem paths.
